Chapter: FactsPartition
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog

 The fact store on a disk that HAS a partition of its own. The refusal side
 is codex/test/apps/disk-facts-gpt-guard (a GPT with no fact partition) and
 codex/test/apps/disk-facts-mbr-guard (an MBR-only disk); this is the side
 where the store finds its region and writes inside it.

 The fixture is 128 KB with an ESP at LBA 34..127 and a fact-store partition
 at 128..222. The ESP is there so a lookup by type GUID has to pick the
 SECOND entry: with one partition on the disk, a lookup that finds the right
 type and a lookup that takes whatever is first produce the same answer, and
 the test would pass for a store that had never learned to read a GUID.

 Five readings, and each one fails differently:

   base    must be 128. This is the whole change. A store that still
           addressed absolutely would answer 0, and 0 is also what a raw
           disk legitimately answers -- which is why the fixture puts the
           partition somewhere a wrong answer cannot coincide with a right
           one.
   limit   must be 95, the partition's sector count. Without it the store
           has no idea where its region ends and a full log writes into
           whatever partition follows.
   count   must reach 1. The refusal tests require it to STAY 0, so this is
           the reading that separates "addresses its partition" from
           "refuses everything", which is the failure mode a guard slides
           into.
   read    the fact must come back through a fresh disk-load, which is the
           only thing that proves the bytes reached the medium at the
           relative address the loader then reads from. A store that wrote
           to the wrong sectors and kept an in-memory copy answers this
           correctly from memory, so the reload is not ceremony.
   sig     sector 1 must still read EFI PART. This is the byte the old
           absolute-addressing store destroyed on Codex's own boot stick,
           and it is checked AFTER a successful write rather than after a
           refusal -- a store that refuses also leaves it intact.

 We say:

Section: Readings

  sig-intact : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  sig-intact (buf) (i) =
    if i >= 8 then True
    else if peek-byte buf i /= list-at [69, 70, 73, 32, 80, 65, 82, 84] i then False
    else sig-intact buf (i + 1)

  shown : Maybe Fact -> Text
  shown (m) =
    when m
      is Just (f) -> f.content
      is None -> "MISSING"

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    let store = disk-load
    in let written = disk-write-fact store DefinitionFact "in-partition" 7
    in let flushed = disk-checkpoint written
    in let reloaded = disk-load
    in act
      hdr <- block-read-sector 1
      print-line-uni ("base " & show (store.base))
      print-line-uni ("limit " & show (store.limit))
      print-line-uni ("count " & show (disk-fact-count flushed))
      print-line-uni ("read " & shown (disk-read-fact reloaded (fact-content-hash "in-partition")))
      print-line-uni ("sig " & (if sig-intact hdr 0 then "intact" else "GONE"))
    end
  end
